    "title": "Chuck Norris, star of 'Walker, Texas Ranger,' dies at 86",
    "text": "Chuck Norris, who starred in \"Walker Texas Ranger\" has died at the age of 86. frederic meylan/Frederic MeylanChuck Norris, the martial arts champion who rose to fame as an action star and became a Texas icon after starring in the popular television series Walker, Texas Ranger, has passed away at 86. Norris was hospitalized after experiencing a medical emergency in Hawaii on Thursday, March 19. His family posted a statement announcing his death on Friday morning on Instagram.Article continues below this ad\"It is with heavy hearts that our family shares the sudden passing of our beloved Chuck Norris yesterday morning,\" the statement reads. \"To the world, he was a martial artist, actor, and a symbol of strength. To us, he was a devoted husband, a loving father and grandfather, an incredible brother, and the heart of our family,\" the statement continued. \"He lived his life with faith, purpose, and an unwavering commitment to the people he loved. Through his work, discipline, and kindness, he inspired millions around the world and left a lasting impact on so many lives.\"The family added, \"While we would like to keep the circumstances private, please know that he was surrounded by his family and was at peace.\"Article continues below this adAfter being encouraged by Steve McQueen to pursue acting, Norris made his feature film debut as a Texas Ranger who battles a weapons dealer and martial arts expert (David Carradine) in the 1983 Sergio Leone-inspired film Lone Wolf McQuade. The role would serve as inspiration for his iconic television role that made him a Texas icon 10 years later. After starring in martial arts films alongside the likes of Bruce Lee, Norris, who was born in Oklahoma, transitioned to television, starring in Walker, Texas Ranger from 1993 to 2001. The show is a piece of Texas culture, as was his role as Cordell Walker. He was made an honorary Texas Ranger in 2010 by Texas Governor Rick Perry for his role and advocacy for Texas law enforcement. Article continues below this adMarch 20, 2026Jamil David is a Culture Reporter for Chron writing about all things Houston and Texas and everything between. Born, raised and educated in the greater Houston area, he previously covered film and television at the Houston Press and entertainment news at Blavity.",
